v1.10.1
fix  : decoding into a ring buffer of `LZ4_decoderRingBufferSize()` could corrupt data (since v1.9.0)
fix  : `LZ4_compress_fast_continue()` produced wrong data when a block overwrote its whole previous block
fix  : `LZ4_compress_destSize_extState()` corrupted data with `acceleration <= 0`, and could read out of bounds (since v1.10.0)
fix  : negative sizes crashed `LZ4_decompress_safe_partial()` (since v1.9.3) and `LZ4_decompress_fast()` (since v1.9.4)
fix  : `LZ4_saveDict()` with a negative `dictSize` copied up to 64 KB into `safeBuffer`, by @arthuz-th
fix  : `LZ4_saveDictHC()` before any compression (new or reset stream) corrupted the next compressed block
fix  : `LZ4_compress_HC_continue_destSize()` corrupted the next block after a call which didn't consume its whole input (since v1.8.0), reported by @freedom-winds
fix  : `LZ4_decompress_safe_partial()` could leave previous `dst` content in decoded data with an invalid offset 0 (since v1.8.3)
fix  : heap overflow with an invalid `blockSizeID` in `LZ4F_compressBegin()` and `LZ4F_writeOpen()`, by @jaypatrickhoward
fix  : `LZ4F_compressBegin()` failing on allocation left an inconsistent frame in progress : it is now invalidated
fix  : `LZ4F_uncompressedUpdate()` mixed with `LZ4F_compressUpdate()` could exceed `LZ4F_compressBound()`
fix  : `LZ4F_compressBegin()` could crash after a previous call failed to allocate its state (custom allocator)
fix  : `LZ4F_uncompressedUpdate()` supports linked blocks (it could produce corrupted frames, without error)
fix  : `LZ4F_compressBegin_usingDict()` ignored the dictionary with independent blocks : each block now uses it, as with a CDict (also multithreaded)
fix  : single-thread `lz4` could truncate concatenated frames with block checksums (wrong decompression hints)
fix  : `--favor-decSpeed` lost after the first block with `-BD`, and ignored with a dictionary
fix  : poor compression by `LZ4_compress_fast_extState_fastReset()` after `LZ4_attach_dictionary()` on a new state
fix  : fast levels could compress some periodic data 30x worse, notably with a dictionary (other data : ~0.3-0.8% better, ~1-8% slower), reported by @martinus
fix  : level 12 very slow on short-period data with linked blocks, detected by OSS-Fuzz
fix  : levels 10+ with `(NULL, 0)` input, reported by @rinsuki
fix  : lz4frame functions check `NULL` arguments, by @louislafosse
fix  : `LZ4F_readOpen()` accepts files smaller than 19 bytes, by @axiqia
fix  : `LZ4F_read()` reports truncated files as an error (`ERROR_io_read`), instead of a clean end of file
fix  : `LZ4F_writeClose()` reports an earlier `LZ4F_write()` failure, instead of success
fix  : lz4frame levels `INT_MIN` and `INT_MIN+1` overflowed, and compressed at default speed instead of maximum acceleration, by @akashdeshmukh-git
fix  : lz4frame `skipChecksums` also skips block checksums of compressed blocks (decompression up to +23% faster)
fix  : C90 compilation of `lz4.c` with `LZ4_HEAPMODE=1` (since v1.10.0)
perf : faster decompression on aarch64 and x86-64 (up to +19% on Neoverse-V2 and Zen 4), by @Nicoshev
perf : `LZ4_FAST_DEC_LOOP` enabled on aarch64 with clang, including Android (up to +9% on Cortex-A5xx), by @Nicoshev and @arpadpanyik-arm
perf : `LZ4_FAST_DEC_LOOP` enabled on riscv64 when unaligned accesses are fast (`-mno-strict-align`, or a `-mcpu` with fast unaligned accesses)
perf : faster compression with reused contexts, for blocks >= 1 KB : linked frames (up to +69%), `LZ4_resetStream_fast()` (up to +94%)
perf : faster compression of 2-4 KB blocks with `LZ4_attach_dictionary()` (+3% to +30% on Zen 4)
perf : 4 KB blocks with `LZ4_attach_HC_dictionary()` : level 2 compresses ~2.5% better, levels 11-12 faster (+12% to +170%), level 10 slower (-3% to -11%)
perf : level 2 compresses better (~0.2-1.5%), at similar speed (-3% to +1% on Zen 4)
perf : faster levels 11 (~+25%) and 12 (~+10%)
perf : `--favor-decSpeed` : faster decompression (~+2-4% at level 12), with fewer sequences
perf : `lz4` multithreaded compression relies on liblz4, reading files in place (memory-mapped, `--[no-]mmap`) : faster at fast levels
api  : lz4frame compression accepts any `dstCapacity` : `LZ4F_compressFrame()` and `LZ4F_compressUpdate()` fail only if the output doesn't fit
api  : `LZ4F_flush()` and `LZ4F_compressEnd()` fill `dstBuffer` or complete : below `LZ4F_compressBound(0)`, invoke them again while they return `dstCapacity`
api  : `LZ4F_ERROR_dstMaxSize_tooSmall` renamed `LZ4F_ERROR_dstCapacity_tooSmall` (former name still supported)
api  : lz4frame : after an error, and outside of a frame, compression operations fail until `LZ4F_compressBegin()`
api  : lz4frame output no longer depends on the compression context's history
api  : new static functions `LZ4F_cctx_size()` and `LZ4F_dctx_size()`, by @felixhandte
api  : new static function `LZ4F_resumeFrame()` : resume decoding from any block start, for random access within frames
api  : `lz4file` : new static functions `LZ4F_seek()` and `LZ4F_tell()`, for random access within compressed files
api  : `LZ4_ACCELERATION_DEFAULT` and `LZ4_ACCELERATION_MAX` are defined in `lz4.h` (static section)
api  : new `LZ4F_preferences_t.nbWorkers` (experimental) : multithreaded lz4frame compression, same output for any `nbWorkers` >= 1 ; `LZ4F_compressBound()` counts jobs instead of blocks, independently of `nbWorkers`
api  : new static function `LZ4F_getMaxNbWorkers()` : tells if liblz4 supports multithreaded compression
api  : `LZ4F_compressOptions_t.stableSrc` : new value `LZ4F_srcStableUntilFlush`, letting worker threads read input in place
api  : reserved fields of `LZ4F_preferences_t` and `LZ4F_compressOptions_t` are now scalars : flatten positional initializers `{ 0, 0, 0 }`
cli  : decodes Firefox's mozlz4 files (`.jsonlz4`, `.mozlz4`), proposed by @UnitedMarsupials
cli  : decodes legacy frames ended by four zero bytes (Linux kernel end marker), requested by @benjarobin
cli  : compressed output no longer depends on multithreading support (`lz4` and `lz4-nomt` produce the same frames, also with `-BD`)
cli  : benchmark (`-b`) measures frames, compressed as `lz4` does with the same options (`-B#`, `-BD`, `-BX`, `-D`, `-T#`, etc.)
cli  : new option `--single-thread` : frames compressed by the main thread alone, in liblz4's single-threaded mode (output can differ from `-T1` with `-BD`)
cli  : custom block sizes (`-B#`, # >= 32) are honored, and compressed single-threaded
cli  : `-T#` : nb of compression threads (the main thread reads and writes files), limited by file size and by a memory budget (`--memory=#`, `LZ4_MEMORY`), with a warning when it reduces `#`
cli  : `-T#` capped by liblz4 (1024 threads, 128 on 32-bit systems), replacing build macro `LZ4_NBWORKERS_MAX` ; `-V` tells if compression is multithreaded
cli  : output buffer no longer grows with the nb of threads : more threads within a memory budget (ex: 22 instead of 13 with `--memory=300M`)
cli  : a source file modified, renamed or replaced during its compression is detected, and kept with `--rm` ; error 54 when the output can't be verified
cli  : `lz4 -l` could hang on Windows with an empty input, as could decoding the resulting legacy stream
cli  : `--rm` keeps the source file after a decompression failure, by @Furan666
cli  : `lz4 -t --rm` no longer removes the tested file, and `--rm` with `stdin` no longer fails nor removes a file named `stdin`, by @Nexory
cli  : `lz4 -t -m` no longer writes decoded content to stdout, reported by @tansy
cli  : `lz4 --list` rejects truncated frames, instead of reporting them as valid, by @jmestwa-coder
cli  : `lz4 --list` no longer reads uninitialized bytes on truncated frame headers, now reported as not recognized, by @akashdeshmukh-git
cli  : numeric arguments that overflow are rejected, instead of silently wrapping around, by @akashdeshmukh-git
cli  : `-b --fast=#` benchmarks a single level, instead of all levels up to -10000 when # > 10000
cli  : legacy format (`-l`) : `--fast=#` selects the same acceleration as the frame format (`--fast=1` was the same as `-1`)
cli  : legacy format (`-l`) : honors `--rm`, and keeps the source file's owner, permissions and modification time
cli  : `-m` : a missing input file no longer distorts the final speed summary of compression
cli  : warning when `--content-size` is used with `stdin`, by @jlee303
cli  : fix benchmark cpu time on Windows with very large files
cli  : `-b` crashed with small block sizes (`-B#`) on large inputs (since v1.7.3)
cli  : `-b -d` could crash with multiple large files, or a frame decoding to more than 2 GB (since v1.9.4), reported by @arshsmith1
cli  : multithreaded decompression could write out of bounds after 2^31 frames (since v1.10.0), by @arshsmith1
build: x86 builds keep branches within 32-byte boundaries (JCC erratum) : faster on Intel cpus of the Skylake family (up to +12% compression, +31% decompression), unchanged on others
build: refactored `make` build, parallel by default
build: `make all install` could fail to link, or install incomplete files, by @hotschmoe
build: cmake : `lz4` supports multithreading on pthread platforms
build: cmake : exported `lz4::lz4` target by @dg0yt, CMake 4 support by @GeorgH93, `CMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE` by @ds-sloth
build: reproducible static library, by @rpurdie
build: dynamic library multithreaded by default, static library single-threaded : `make lib-mt` / `lib-nomt`, cmake and meson options
build: `make install` installs the libraries already built (ex: `make lib-mt && make install`)
build: meson fixes, by @grobian, @bgilbert and @eli-schwartz
build: AIX support, by @shubhamhii
build: pkg-config `License` field, by @iwamatsu
build: public headers compile with clang C++ modules (`-fmodules`), reported by @doraorak
build: new build macro `LZ4_WILDCOPY64`, to tune decompression on targets like wasm, proposed by @JCash
build: `xxhash.c` compiles without `-Wbad-function-cast` warnings, by @brainy
port : RISC-V with `Zicclsm` uses unaligned memory accesses, by @Polaris-911
doc  : ring buffer conditions of `LZ4_decompress_safe_continue()`
doc  : frame format : Linux kernel legacy last block, by @Artoria2e5

